---
import IndustryPage from "../../components/page/IndustryPage.astro";

export const prerender = false;
---

<IndustryPage
	slug="professional-services"
	eyebrow="Professional firms"
	title="IT, HR and digital presence for Ontario law and accounting firms"
	description="Matter-based permissions, conflict-checked onboarding, retention that holds up, and the software operations behind a boutique or mid-size professional firm."
	h1="Access control is a professional obligation here, not an IT preference."
	heroLead="Boutique and mid-size law firms, notaries, paralegal practices, accounting and tax practices. Everywhere else, over-permissioned files are untidy. In a professional firm they're a conflict, a privilege problem, or a regulator's question."
	fundingText="Practice management and document system work often qualifies for government funding — federal, and provincial depending on where you operate."
	directAnswer="Professional firms carry an obligation most small businesses don't: the requirement to demonstrate, not merely intend, that client information was accessible only to the people entitled to see it. That turns ordinary IT decisions — file permissions, email retention, who can search what — into questions with professional consequences, and it's the reason generic small-business setups sit poorly in a firm."
	differencesTitle="Three constraints that reshape everything else."
	differences={[
		{
			heading: "Permissions are matter-based, not department-based.",
			body: "Ordinary access control asks which team you're on. A firm has to ask which matters you're on, which changes constantly, and which people must be walled off from — an ethical screen is only real if the system actually enforces it. Most off-the-shelf configurations can't express this without being set up deliberately for it.",
		},
		{
			heading: "Conflict checks belong in onboarding.",
			body: "A lateral hire brings a history. Running the conflict check before their access is provisioned rather than after is a small sequencing decision with a large consequence, and it's the sort of thing that gets skipped when onboarding is improvised.",
		},
		{
			heading: "Retention is a rule, not a preference.",
			body: "Documents have to be kept for a defined period and, just as importantly, disposed of after it. Keeping everything forever is not a retention policy — it's a liability that compounds, and it's what a discovery request or a regulator finds.",
		},
	]}
	pillars={[
		{
			label: "Business software",
			body: "Practice or matter management chosen and configured so permissions follow matters, with document storage structured to match. Time and billing connected to accounting. Where AI is used, it's constrained to what the individual user is already entitled to see — which is the whole question in a firm, and the one most AI rollouts get wrong.",
		},
		{
			label: "Back office",
			body: "Onboarding sequenced so the conflict check precedes access. Permission changes that follow people as they move between matters rather than accumulating over a career. Offboarding that closes access cleanly and preserves the record. Payroll and benefits run to schedule alongside it.",
		},
		{
			label: "Digital presence",
			body: "Practice-area pages that answer the question a prospective client is actually asking, with intake that routes to the right person and doesn't create a conflict problem before anyone has checked. Professional, quiet, and fast — the register your clients expect.",
		},
	]}
	faq={[
		{
			q: "Can you enforce ethical screens in our document system?",
			a: "Yes, when the system supports it and it's configured deliberately from the start. We'll tell you plainly if your current setup can't express matter-level restriction without being rebuilt — that's a real finding and better heard early than during a complaint.",
		},
		{
			q: "How do you handle a lateral hire's conflict check?",
			a: "It goes before provisioning, not after. Their accounts, matter access and document permissions are only issued once the check clears. Sequencing costs nothing and removes an entire category of problem.",
		},
		{
			q: "Is it safe to use AI tools in a firm?",
			a: "Only after permissions are correct — and that's the actual work, not the licence. An assistant that can summarise your document store will happily summarise a file the user was never entitled to open. We fix access control first, then scope what the tools can reach, then decide with you whether the remaining use is worth having. Sometimes it isn't.",
		},
		{
			q: "Do you understand our retention obligations?",
			a: "We build and run what you specify. We're not your professional advisor on how long a particular record must be kept — that's a rule you or your regulator sets. What we do is make sure the system enforces it rather than relying on someone remembering.",
		},
		{
			q: "We already have an IT provider who knows our practice software.",
			a: "Then keep them. We'd take software operations, identity and lifecycle, licensing and vendor coordination, and coordinate with them on the practice system. We'll say plainly if we think the overlap isn't worth paying for twice.",
		},
	]}
	ctaTitle="Tell us how a matter gets set up."
	ctaLead="Who gets access, when, and who decides. That conversation surfaces most of what we'd want to change."
/>
